<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TextBook"]/comps/li[@Class="RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Investigation.CompProperties_RouteEvidence"]</xpath>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TextBook"]/comps</xpath>
      <value>
        <!-- THE 3000 THAT WAS HERE IS GONE, AND NOTHING CHANGED BY REMOVING IT. It was the
             default on CompProperties_RouteEvidence in C# written out a second time, which was
             harmless while this was the only book carrying the component. 0.13.0-dev ships the
             company's own journal with the same component, so leaving it would have made two
             copies of a tuned number in two files that nothing keeps in agreement. The value now
             lives once, where it is declared. -->
        <li Class="RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Investigation.CompProperties_RouteEvidence" />
      </value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

  <!-- THE thingClass BINDING IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. It lives in
       `Investigation/RecordBookClassBinding.cs`, at StaticConstructorOnStartup.

       Owner report, 2026-10-06: the two starting journals are "named wrong and have differ
       information in the "i" write up saying incorrectly that one is about nutrition and the
       othert is about aiming". The cause is that `Verse.Book` overrides `LabelNoCount`,
       `LabelNoParenthesis` and `DescriptionFlavor` without calling base, so no comp can reach a
       book's label or description, `CompRouteEvidence.TransformLabel` has been dead code since
       the day it was written. The fix is a thingClass deriving from Book that puts those chains
       back.

       A first attempt put that binding here, and `check-compliance` rule 3 refused it: Core
       declares thingClass on the ABSTRACT parent `BookBase`, so an xpath on TextBook's own
       thingClass matches nothing and the only patch that works is a Replace, and a Replace takes
       ownership of a def where the last mod to load wins. THE RULE WAS RIGHT AND THE PATCH WAS
       WRONG. Patching BookBase instead would have handed our class to Novel, Schematic and Tome.

       Binding in code at startup is strictly better than any xpath could be, which is the same
       argument `FixtureTellService` already makes for attaching its comp there:

         * it runs after inheritance resolves and after every mod's defs load, so it sees the real
           value rather than the authored one;
         * it can REFUSE to clobber. If some other mod has already changed TextBook's class, the
           binding leaves it alone and says so in the log, instead of winning or losing silently by
           load order;
         * it is additive in the sense rule 3 means: no def is taken over, nothing is replaced, and
           removing this mod removes the behaviour completely.

       `check-record-book-class.py` (checker 27) asserts the binding exists, refuses a destructive
       operation in this file, and asserts every route that mints a record book marks it as the
       company's, because an unmarked book makes the whole bridge a no-op. -->
</Patch>
